# Cline Rules — jsmastery Project

These rules are enforced on every Cline task in this project. They exist to prevent the mistakes documented in `docs/LESSONS_LEARNED.md`.

## Core Principles

### 1. Read everything before acting
- **Never edit code before reading all relevant source files, git history, and prior conversations.**
- The user has explicitly said: *"read all chat and tasks history related to this project"* and *"You never listened to my instructions read everything."*
- Use subagents for broad exploration to avoid wasting context.
- Check git history (`git log`, `git show`) to understand what previous changes did before making your own.

### 2. No claims without verification
- **Never claim a fix is complete without building and testing on the emulator.**
- The user has explicitly said: *"run the build and run it in the emulator don't just claim it's fixed we have been over this terrible behavior of yours."*
- Every fix must pass: `tsc` → build → emulator launch → manual test on device.
- "Build succeeds" ≠ "feature works." Always verify the actual user-facing behavior.

### 3. One issue at a time
- **Fix one problem, verify it, then move to the next.**
- Do not touch unrelated files when fixing one issue. The user said: *"IDK why it touched the model generation scripts"* — this must never happen again.
- If you discover a new issue while fixing something else, note it but do not fix it in the same pass.

### 4. Test with real, diverse data
- **Test with 5+ real, diverse subscriptions** (rare companies, not just Netflix/Spotify).
- The user said: *"try all kind of companies add at least 5 rare subscriptions when you test because the claims of success are just piling on the errors."*
- Verify icon quality is brand-correct, not random images.

### 5. Commit before risky changes
- **Git commit first, then edit.** The user said: *"if you need to delete files just git commit first."*
- This ensures you can always revert if something breaks.

### 6. No model training without wiring verification
- **Never train models while the app wiring is broken.**
- The user said: *"You made me spend thousands of dollars on AI training and brought me down in a rabbit hole for the upscaling."*
- Before any training: verify the inference pipeline works end-to-end with existing models.
- Training is frozen per `docs/plan.md` — do not unfreeze without explicit user instruction.

### 7. 3-strike safeguard
- **If 3 consecutive fix attempts fail, STOP immediately.**
- Do not keep trying the same approach.
- Provide a detailed resume to the user, git commit, and brainstorm a new solution.
- Update `docs/plan.md` with the new approach before continuing.

### 8. No tool-call spirals
- **If a tool call is rejected by the harness, do not repeat the same command.**
- Try a different approach or tool. If it keeps failing, escalate to the user.
- The user said: *"Don't repeat tool call for no reasons."*

### 9. Update documentation
- **After every fix, update the relevant documentation.**
- Keep `docs/plan.md` as the single source of truth for the execution plan.
- Delete stale docs, not create new ones alongside them.

### 10. Follow the phased workflow
- Use the workflow in `docs/CLINE_WORKFLOW.md`:
  **Analyse → Edit → Lint/Syntax → Commit → Build+Emulator → Test → Doc/Hygiene → Commit → Repeat**
- Gate after each risky tranche: `tsc` + build + emulator smoke.
